More to the point, IMO, is the fact that just *one* random roll at
the very beginning has a huge impact on the difficulty of the
entire game.  Just for example, if object detection is yellow and
full healing is light green, finding one light blue potion in
Sokoban can mean that suddenly your character has five times as
many hitpoints as would be normal at that stage of the game, before
even reaching Minetown.  On the other hand, with a slightly
different dice roll at the beginning, full and extra healing and
gain ability could all be white potions and virtually impossible to
alchemize.

Randomness is built into NetHack, but most of it is done so that
you are at the mercy of not one roll but the cumulative effect of
many dice rolls over the entire course of the game.  This tempers
things somewhat, so that even rolls don't make the game too easy
and bad rolls don't make it too ridiculously hard.

There are a couple of exceptions, but IMO those are the problematic
cases.  For example, in vanilla, smoky potions can be something
very easy to alchemize and worth drinking for its own sake (e.g.,
full healing), or they can be something difficult to alchemize that
you'd never drink if it weren't smoky.  I'd like to see this
game-changer toned down considerably, perhaps by making the chance
of getting a djinni from a smoky potion (or from a magic lamp, for
that matter) inversely proportional to the square or maybe even the
cube of the number of wishes you've already received in the game,
so that a handful of smoky potions in the early game can still get
you an early wish, but a stack of eighty of them later in the game
doesn't get you a ridiculous, game-debalancing quanitity of wishes.

> With alchemy I think another Nethack issue should be adjusted; if
